  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/8/89/Strange_Tales_Vol_1_118.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Johnny Storm finishes up a practice session before heading off to go bowling with some friends.&nbsp; At the same time, the Wizard has remained a model prisoner, while collecting the materials he needs, but at last he gasses a guard, takes his place using disguise techniques, and uses a crude version of a new anti-gravity device to fly over the prison yard wall and escape.&nbsp; He imagines how easy it will be to use it to carry off heavy loot such as safes.&nbsp; No place will be safe from the Wizard now!\n\nDays later, Johnny receives a letter inviting him to do a one-man exhibition for charity over closed-circuit TV, to which he declares \"I may become the Captain Kangaroo of the teenage set!\"&nbsp; When Johnny arrives, the man has him perform flame stunt after stunt until his flame is all burned out.&nbsp; Then the Wizard reveals himself!&nbsp; He was disguised and had tricked Johnny into using all his flame!&nbsp; He quickly subdues Johnny and uses another disguise, a \"cold-flame unit,\" and his new anti-gravity disks to mimic his appearance and ability to fly.&nbsp; He'll hide out as the Human Torch in plain sight!\n\nReturning to the house Johnny shares with his sister Sue, the Wizard gasses her too.&nbsp; None will know that he's taken Johnny's place now!&nbsp; But just in case, he flies to the Baxter Building and tells Reed and Ben that he and Sue are going on a little vacation for a while.&nbsp; Now, the Wizard can work uninterrupted in the Storms' own house to perfect his anti-gravity device.&nbsp; It's perfect!&nbsp; He imagines all the ways that he'll be able to \"become a world power, single-handed...!\"\n\nWhen Johnny and Sue eventually wake up, they find themselves in an enclosed space, but they manage to find a small air hole, through which Johnny sends out an FF flare, and Reed and Ben come running to the rescue!&nbsp; Now freed, Johnny flies back to his house to confront the Wizard, who had planned to use his anti-gravity device to send Johnny on a one-way trip into space, but it is melted by the Human Torch's nova flame, so he uses his last device on himself and floats to apparent freedom, knowing that the Torch's flame has run out after the nova blast.\n\nBut the Wizard's device malfunctions, and he finds he can't stop his ascent!&nbsp; He floats off to \"the section of sky where there's no air to breathe!\"&nbsp; The Torch knows the Wizard is clever, and this may not actually be the end of him, but it is for this month at any rate!",
  "grade_one_liner": "The Wizard develops anti-gravity but fails to remember the events of issue 105.\nOne memorable invention struggles to survive a tidal wave of recycled storytelling.",
  "subtitle": "Anti-Gravity, Anti-Originality",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: left | AntiGravDevice | width=14%] The Wizard's last appearance was his ill-fated team-up with Paste Pot Pete in the pages of [LINK:1963:ST_110_torch1|ST #110torch|2026-09-17] (unless you count a single panel in [LINK:1963:ST_112_torch0|ST #112|2026-11-04]).&nbsp; But in this story, he is shown to have invented some sort of anti-gravity device, a development that may prove more significant than the actual plot surrounding it.&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: Of course, this is precisely what the Wizard comes to be known for much later.]&nbsp; Together with his ability to mimic the appearance of others (something he's done a couple of times already), he soars over the prison yard walls and escapes.\n\nThe idea that Johnny would fall for the old [SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=\"You could be a <b>star</b>!\"] trick is laughable, especially after the events of [LINK:1962:FF_9|FF #9|2026-05-01] (published about 15 months earlier), where the FF's money problems were supposedly to be fixed by an offer for the four of them to star in a movie.\n\nThe Wizard's plan to get the Human Torch to use up all his flame before finally revealing himself [SPAN: severity=info; text=wasn't terrible], but like so many of the other events in this story, it wasn't exactly <i>inspired</i> either!\n[ASSET: center | JohnnyFallsForIt | width=67%]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: right | PrisonersWithNoEscape | width=21%] By the end of page 5, he's once again taken prisoner of the Wizard, who immediately sets out to impersonate him, this time able to \"fly\" via the use of his new anti-gravity device.\n[BR]\n[BR] Imprisoning the Human Torch rather than outright killing him shows that he didn't learn his lesson after the events of [LINK:1962:ST_105_torch0|ST #105|2026-05-01], where he captured both Johnny <i>and</i> his sister Sue.\n[BR]\n[BR] For someone who is supposed to be brilliant, he sure has a hard time figuring out how to play the role of \"villain\" properly!\n\nAnd isn't it worth asking the question:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=How on Earth does one even <i>transport</i> two adults to a billboard on the top of Times Square?]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Presumably with the help of his anti-gravity device!)]\n\n[ASSET: left | QuittingTheFF | width=19%] After surprising Sue (and gassing her too), his plan to keep the other two FF members at bay while he uses their house to perfect his anti-gravity device is [SPAN: severity=error; text=weak.]&nbsp; Does he really think that Reed will buy that Johnny and Sue simply want a break from the \"togetherness\" (as the panel indicates)?\n[BR]\n[BR] Even granting that Johnny is a hot-headed teenager, Reed knows that <b>Sue is not</b> (and that she would surely have mentioned such a decision beforehand).\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=This was simply inferior storytelling!]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And also note that the Baxter Building once again only has four upper floors in the panel, as opposed to the five readers know it <i>actually </i>has.)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nNaturally, the captured Human Torch and Invisible Girl find a way to summon the other members of the FF...\n[ASSET: center | SummoningTheFF | width=67%]\n\nWhat follows is about as formulaic as you can imagine:\n[HANG] * Reed and Ben arrive\n[HANG] * The Thing smashes open their enclosure (ironically, a large ad for safety matches)\n[HANG] * Johnny flies off to re-engage the Wizard\n[HANG] * The Wizard plans to send the Human Torch on a one-way trip to the stratosphere\n[HANG] * But the Human Torch melts his weapons with his nova flame (using up the last of his flames)\n[HANG] * Meaning the Wizard must make his escape using his last anti-grav device\n[HANG] * Which of course malfunctions...\n[HANG] * The Wizard floats off into the upper atmosphere, unable to breathe...\n\n[ASSET: center | BestLaidPlans | width=67%]\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; text=The Wizard is seemingly doomed], though Johnny notes in the final panel that the Wizard is clever enough that he may actually find a way to save himself.\n\nFor now, however, the story closes, and the [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=day is saved.]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\n<i>Ugh</i>, this was an [SPAN: severity=error; text=uninspired effort] that used a criminal who continues to claim he is one of the smartest people alive, [SPAN: severity=info; text=but without enough sense to actually <i>kill</i> his enemies.]&nbsp; (I mean <i>seriously</i>, if your plan is to rule the world, don't you think it might be necessary to <b>bump off</b> a few people along the way?)\n\nFor a man who was introduced back in [LINK:1962:ST_102_torch0|ST #102|2026-04-30] as a brilliant inventor and escape artist, it's hard to imagine how he mentally calculates that trapping the Human Torch and Invisible Girl in a sealed space means that they can never escape, [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=<i>especially</i> after he did <i>exactly that</i>] back in [LINK:1962:ST_105_torch0|ST #105|2026-05-01] (which I'm guessing Stan simply hoped readers would no longer remember -- it's the only explanation!)\n\nLet's face it -- this was little more than a modified rehash of [LINK:1962:ST_105_torch0|ST #105|2026-05-01] (published barely a year earlier), where the Wizard captures both Johnny and Sue after escaping from prison (<i>sound familiar?</i>).&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=The only real wrinkle this time was his new anti-gravity device.]\n\nThe plot was so uninspired that the resemblance stopped feeling coincidental and started feeling recycled, and I barely made it to the halfway point of the story before I began checking the page count.\n\n<i>Yes</i>, the anti-gravity device was an idea worth noting, so the story doesn't sink down into oblivion, and <i>yes</i>, there is the matter at the end of him floating off into the thin atmosphere, seemingly doomed (leaving readers to wonder what will become of him).&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=Admittedly, both of these were good things.]\n\nBut aside from that, the very <i>idea</i> that he could imprison the Human Torch and the Invisible Girl inside an enclosed space, then believe the pair would simply stop trying to escape and patiently wait to die of \"slow starvation\" (as he says on page 8)...\n\nThis is the [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=epitome of idiocy!]\n\nThere's simply no way to spin this:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=This was a major yawner....]",
  "highlight": "The Wizard unveils his new anti-gravity device, but Johnny and Sue end up trapped in a plot that feels strangely familiar."
}